<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Wild bytes ~ carry what cannot be re-made, re-found what can

''Two kinds of bytes reach a reader, and the house owes them opposite treatment.'' Bytes the house can
re-make — its own state, its own carriers, anything a rite re-founds — answer to the CURRENT grammar and
nothing else. Bytes the house can never re-make — history, bytes from outside, a month of old transcripts —
answer to whatever grammar wrote them, and that grammar may no longer stand.

> ''The house CARRIES wild bytes UNREAD and FLAGGED, and never repair-tolerates state it can re-found.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-line>>

!! The line ~ who can re-make these bytes

The test runs on one question: ''could a rite make these bytes again?''

| |! re-foundable state |! wild bytes |
| examples | a carrier under `bags/`, a vessel substrate, a registration, a signing domain | an old transcript, a peer's foreign payload, history written under a retired frame |
| the grammar it answers to | the current one, alone | the one that wrote it |
| a reading that fails | the bytes RE-FOUND under the current grammar | the bytes stay CARRIED, unread, and FLAGGED |
| a reader's tolerance | MUST NOT exist — a tolerated old shape keeps the old shape alive | MAY exist, scoped to that reader, named at its seat |

The house MUST NOT carry a repair path, fallback, or alias for state it can re-found: re-founding costs one
rite, and a tolerance costs every later reader a second grammar to hold.
A reader of wild bytes MUST NOT silently coerce what it cannot type. It carries the bytes as received and
names them flagged — a demotion the operator sees, never a drop and never a guess.
A reader that tolerates a retired shape MUST scope that tolerance to bytes it never authored, and its
docblock MUST name which wild source the tolerance serves.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/parse-dont-validate>>

!! Parse, don't validate ~ the edge types the bytes once

''A reader turns wild bytes into a typed reading at the edge, exactly once'' (Alexis King, //Parse, don't
validate//). Everything past the edge holds the typed reading and never re-inspects the raw bytes; a
validator that answers yes-or-no and hands the raw bytes onward leaves every later reader to re-decide, and
each one decides a little differently.

A reading the edge cannot type takes no default shape. It stays carried beside a named reason — the same
floor the ahu quoteblock and graceful parsing hold one layer over
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor">>,
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing#/recovery-policy">>): water never lies about
structure.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-alpha-line>>

!! With the alpha line ~ one law seen from two sides

''The alpha line rules no back-compat and no shims'' for anything the house authored: a ruling that obsoletes
a shape deletes the old reading whole, and a pre-ruling carrier re-founds rather than being re-read
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/failure-states/protective-optional#/law">>).

''Wild bytes mark the one place that line does not reach'', and the reason stays structural rather than
lenient: no rite re-makes a transcript somebody already spoke, so "re-found it" names no act. The two laws
partition the corpus by one question and never overlap — ''authored → re-found; wild → carry and flag''. A
tolerance found anywhere else reads as a shim wearing this law's name.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! Prior art ~ the robustness principle, measured

* ''RFC 9413, Maintaining Robust Protocols'' (Thomson & Schinazi) — the IETF's own reckoning with
  Postel's //be liberal in what you accept//. Tolerance of malformed input does not stay tolerance: peers
  come to depend on it, the protocol's real grammar drifts away from its written one, and the cure names
  ACTIVE maintenance — specify the error handling, and keep the tolerated set from growing.
* ''Hyrum's law'' — with enough consumers, every observable behaviour of an interface gets depended on.
  A tolerance a reader extends to re-foundable state becomes, in time, a contract nobody wrote. Scoping
  tolerance to bytes the house cannot re-make bounds what Hyrum's law can capture.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/standing-instance>>

!! The standing instance ~ the turn harvester

`packages/lararium-mesh/src/capture/turn-harvest.ts` reads a month of real turns written under whatever the
frame said that week. It keeps its retired-head tolerance — a retired sigil head stays readable there —
under this law: those transcripts form wild bytes, no rite re-makes them, and refusing a retired head would
read a turn that fired its instrument as a turn that fired nothing. Its own docblock states the same cut:
//a corpus states one grammar; a reader tolerates every grammar it will meet//. `bags/` carries current
pono alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

